items.proposed:
Rust owns parsing, validation, rendering, diagnostics, interaction state, authoring logic, storage, access, publication and signup rules. Handwritten JavaScript is allowed only in:

- the VS Code adapter: activation, commands, webview wiring; behaviour comes from the editor service;
- Cloudflare Worker adapters: routing, bindings and identity hand-off; rules, including the signup endpoint's consent and validation (ARC-55), run in Rust compiled to Wasm;
- an embedded VS Code-style source editor on RichView server pages (Publish, Cowork), loaded only for a signed-in author editing their own item (INT-26, FEA-BR-27): a third-party editor library, such as Monaco or CodeMirror (the choice is open), with handwritten mounting glue. It edits and highlights source only; validation and rendering before save run on the server in Rust. No report or report runtime ever loads it;
- generated wasm-bindgen glue and worker bootstraps;
- a narrow landing-page animation layer, where JavaScript is the correct repair (S27-653); it never re-implements shared Rust behaviour;
- development tooling that never ships with the product: tests, verification, benchmarks, evidence and review tools such as the lockdown app (ARC-63 to ARC-65), which may also use Python, R or shell.

#70's JavaScript, merged as it is (INT-52), stays outside this allowlist until the rewrite reaches Publish and ports it to Rust; how the gate's check treats it until then is open. Tooling reaches the product only through its binaries and generated APIs and holds no product rule. Each handwritten JavaScript file in a product path names its host reason in its header, and a gate checks product paths against this allowlist. Custom TypeScript components embedded in a report are a possible later extension (INT-28) that would come with their own validation, sandboxing and skills; they are not part of the rewrite, and this allowlist gains an entry for them only when that extension is decided.
